Find the PSP's memory outside the 4-6 GB band when the band is full

On iOS, MemoryMap_Setup does not let the system place the emulated PSP's
memory: it tries a base every 8 MB from 4 GB to 6 GB and builds its views at
fixed offsets from it (Memory_TryBase). In an app that has been running a
while, that band fills with memory the app does not control (Metal's
IOAccelerator regions, IOSurfaces, malloc's small and large zones), and once
no base is left the boot fails with "MemoryMap_Setup: Failed finding a memory
base". Measured in Retro Pal (Debug memory map at boot): 66 of 256 bases free
when the PSP is the first game after launch, 12 after one short game on each
of the other consoles, and a long session reached none (every PSP game failed
to boot until the app was killed).

Nothing on the interpreter needs the base in that band: on iOS memory is
reached as base + (address & MEMVIEW32_MASK), a full 64-bit add, and the ARM64
JIT, which on iOS never runs (the port forces the IR interpreter without JIT),
already turns pointerify off by itself for a base not aligned to 4 GB
(Arm64IRJit.cpp). So, only after every base in the band has failed, ask the
system for a free range of the size the views span (Memory_ViewSpan: the end
of the highest view, with the same skips and masking as Memory_TryBase),
release it, and build the views there. A first boot in a fresh app still takes
the band exactly as before.

Checked on the host with MemoryMap_Setup, Memory_TryBase and the view table
taken verbatim from this file, on iOS settings, against a simulated address
space: band partly taken, the band base is found as before; band full, the
unpatched code fails and the patched code places the views above it, and every
PSP address (RAM, its uncached mirror, VRAM and its mirror, scratchpad, the
64 MB models' extra RAM) lands on mapped memory.

Applied at build time by Vendor/ppsspp-ios/build.sh.

diff --git a/Core/MemMap.cpp b/Core/MemMap.cpp
index a7b5cae..49b8e3b 100644
--- a/Core/MemMap.cpp
+++ b/Core/MemMap.cpp
@@ -41,6 +41,10 @@
 #include "Core/MIPS/JitCommon/JitCommon.h"
 #include "Common/Thread/ParallelLoop.h"
 
+#if PPSSPP_ARCH(ARM64) && PPSSPP_PLATFORM(IOS)
+#include <mach/mach.h>
+#endif
+
 namespace Memory {
 
 // The base pointer to the auto-mirrored arena.
@@ -135,6 +139,23 @@ static bool SkipView(MemMapSetupFlags flags, u32 viewFlags) {
 	return false;
 }
 
+#if PPSSPP_ARCH(ARM64) && PPSSPP_PLATFORM(IOS)
+// How far past base the views Memory_TryBase creates reach: the end of the
+// highest one, with the same skips and the same masking.
+static size_t Memory_ViewSpan(MemMapSetupFlags flags) {
+	size_t span = 0;
+	for (int i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(views); i++) {
+		const MemoryView &view = views[i];
+		if (view.size == 0 || SkipView(flags, view.flags) || CanIgnoreView(view))
+			continue;
+		const size_t end = (size_t)(view.virtual_address & MEMVIEW32_MASK) + view.size;
+		if (end > span)
+			span = end;
+	}
+	return span;
+}
+#endif
+
 static bool Memory_TryBase(MemMapSetupFlags flags) {
 	// OK, we know where to find free space. Now grab it!
 	// We just mimic the popular BAT setup.
@@ -255,6 +276,27 @@ bool MemoryMap_Setup(MemMapSetupFlags flags) {
 				return true;
 			}
 		}
+#if PPSSPP_ARCH(ARM64) && PPSSPP_PLATFORM(IOS)
+		// The 4-6 GB band fills up in a long-running app (Metal and malloc take
+		// it over time), and nothing on the IR interpreter needs the base
+		// there: memory is reached as base + (address & MEMVIEW32_MASK), and
+		// the ARM64 JIT turns pointerify off by itself for an unaligned base.
+		// So let the system pick a free range of the size the views span, and
+		// build the views there.
+		for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 4; attempt++) {
+			const size_t span = Memory_ViewSpan(flags);
+			vm_address_t probe = 0;
+			if (vm_allocate(mach_task_self(), &probe, span, VM_FLAGS_ANYWHERE) != KERN_SUCCESS) {
+				break;
+			}
+			vm_deallocate(mach_task_self(), probe, span);
+			base = (u8 *)probe;
+			if (Memory_TryBase(flags)) {
+				INFO_LOG(Log::MemMap, "Memory base at %p, outside the 4-6 GB band (full).", base);
+				return true;
+			}
+		}
+#endif
 		ERROR_LOG(Log::MemMap, "MemoryMap_Setup: Failed finding a memory base.");
 		return false;
 	}
